<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Irineu Evangelista de Sousa, Viscount of Mauá</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1813">1813</a>–<a href="/years/1889">1889</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Viscount of Mauá, Baron of Mauá</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Irineu Evangelista de Sousa, Viscount of Mauá, was a Brazilian industrialist, banker and entrepreneur. He built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>'s first railway, opened near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a> in 1854, and invested in shipyards, gas lighting, steamship lines and banks in Brazil, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>. His business empire collapsed in the 1870s, but he is remembered as a pioneer of industrialisation in a slave-based agrarian economy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/irineu-evangelista-de-sousa-viscount-of-maua.jpg" alt="Irineu Evangelista de Sousa, Viscount of Mauá" width="320" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joaquim Insley Pacheco (1830-1912); derivative work by Kingpin13 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maua_00.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sousa was born on 28 December 1813 on his father's ranch in the village of Nossa Senhora da Conceição do Arroio Grande, near the southern border of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, then part of Jaguarão in the captaincy of São Pedro do <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande-do-sul">Rio Grande do Sul</a>. He was the second son of João Evangelista de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avila">Ávila</a> e Sousa and Mariana de Jesus Batista, a family of small ranchers with ancestry from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azores">Azores</a>; his paternal grandfather, Manuel Jerônimo de Sousa, had held a land grant from the Portuguese crown.</p><p>In 1818 his father was murdered while travelling with a herd of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, and the family's income fell. When his mother married again in 1821 her new husband would not take her children, and at about nine Irineu was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a> by his uncle José Batista de Carvalho, a ship's captain. His mother had taught him to read and count; whether he had any schooling is doubtful, as his biographers Anyda Marchant and Cláudio Ganns note. Left in Rio while his uncle sailed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, he worked as a clerk in a warehouse.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Sousa became one of the richest men in the world: by 1867 his wealth exceeded the annual budget of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire</a>, and in 1871 The New York Times called him the Rothschild of the South American continent. At his peak he controlled eight of the ten largest companies in the country, the other two being state-owned, and his banking interests reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>. He established the modern Banco do Brasil, founded the first bank in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a>, Banco Mauá y Cia, and financed much economic activity, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> planting.</p><p>He began the Mauá Railroad in 1852, ran steamboats on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a> and Guaíba rivers, commissioned the first submarine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> cable between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and installed gas street lights in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a>. He was made baron in 1854 and viscount with grandeur in 1874. His fortunes declined with the Empire after the Paraguayan War, and by his death on 21 October 1889 he had lost most of his wealth.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In an economy led by landowners who relied on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and exports, Sousa defended the free market, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberalism">liberalism</a>, industrialisation and the abolition of slavery. He is the patron of the Ministry of Transport, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>'s merchant navy day falls on his birthday for his work at the Ponta d'Areia shipyard, and Praça Mauá in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a> and the Baron of Mauá International Bridge between Jaguarão and Río Branco, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a>, bear his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Irineu Evangelista de Sousa, Viscount of Mauá” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By mid-century Brazil was producing around half of the world's coffee, and the proceeds financed the modernisation of Rio de Janeiro, the first railways, beginning with the <b>Mauá</b> line of 1854, and the military effort that sustained the Paraguayan War.</blockquote><a href="/empire/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Jaguarão, in what is now <a href="/countries/uruguay">Uruguay</a>. Died in Petrópolis, in what is now <a href="/countries/brazil">Brazil</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-32.57,-53.38&amp;year=1862" aria-label="Jaguarão on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3180 3347 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Irineu Evangelista de Sousa, Viscount of Mauá"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3630 3617h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3630 3617h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-32.57,-53.38&amp;year=1862">What was here in 1862? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
